-- 0196: the 107 construction-arc notes in clean, short sentences (≤ 3, ≤ 45 words): Arabic only as «quotes» with the
-- verse after them, grammar terms in plain words (verbal noun, particle …), same facts and sources.
-- Rewrite ar_ling_lemma_arc.boundary_note_en (live rows) into short, clean English.
-- Guarded: each UPDATE applies only if the note is still the one read on 2026-10-07.
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It is always forbidden or blamed: the other god is set beside God, not in His place. Ibn ʿĀshūr (on 17:22): leaving this is the first step toward the next life. The Lisān: whatever is worshipped besides God is a god to its worshipper.' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:akhar' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Always forbidden or blamed: لَا تَجْعَلْ, لَا تَدْعُ, الَّذِي جَعَلَ. مَعَ is a place-word (ẓarf), not a particle: the other god is put next to God, not in His place. 6:19 has the plural (آلِهَةً أُخْرَىٰ); 18:14 says the same with مِن دُونِهِ — "we will never call on any god besides Him". Ibn ʿĀshūr at 17:22 (p.65): leaving this is the first step of anyone who wants the next life. Lisān (p.7028): whatever is taken as worshipped besides Him is an إِلَٰه to the one who takes it.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Al-Rāghib: the word should have no plural, since only God is worshipped. The Lisān: the idols were called gods for what their worshippers believed, not for what they are. For Pharaoh''s gods, Ibn ʿAbbās read «وَإِلَاهَتَكَ» (7:127), "and your worship".' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:alihah_idafa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'al-Rāghib (p.82): إِلَٰه should not have a plural, since there is no one worshipped but Him; the Arabs made it plural because they believed in many. Lisān (p.7028): الآلِهَة are the idols, named after what their worshippers believed, not what they are. So the plural is nearly always tied to its worshippers — "our gods", "your gods" — mostly in the mouths of idolaters defending them. 7:127 وَآلِهَتَكَ is Pharaoh''s gods; Ibn ʿAbbās read وَإِلَاهَتَكَ, "and your worship" (Lisān).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'This is the only verse where anyone says "I am a god" of himself. Ibn ʿĀshūr (on 21:29): it is said of the angels only as a supposition, since they never would. It is aimed at those who called them gods.' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:claim' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The only place where someone says of himself إِنِّي إِلَٰهٌ. Ibn ʿĀshūr at 21:29 (p.50): it is said of the angels as a supposition only — they would never say it — and it is aimed at those who claimed godhood for them. مِن دُونِهِ = besides Him, as in the اتَّخَذَ arc.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Here «فِي» attaches to the word "god" itself, which carries the sense "worshipped" (al-Zamakhsharī on 43:84). It does not put God inside a place. Ibn ʿĀshūr: it keeps godhood in heaven and on earth for God alone.' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:fi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Here فِي hangs on إِلَٰه itself, because the word carries the sense "worshipped": al-Zamakhsharī at 43:84 — the name is given the sense of "the One worshipped" or "the Owner", so the place-phrase attaches to it. It does not put God inside a place. Ibn ʿĀshūr (p.268): it keeps godhood in heaven and on earth for God alone, against those who made the angels and the idols partners.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Mostly this is the true God, often followed by "one". Al-Zamakhsharī: others may be called "Lord of people", but «إِلَٰهِ النَّاسِ» (114:3) is God''s alone. It can also name a false god, like the calf (20:88) or Pharaoh''s mocking "god of Moses" (28:38).' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:idafa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Joined to the ones who worship (or should): إِلَٰهُكُمْ, إِلَٰهُنَا, إِلَٰهَ آبَائِكَ, إِلَٰهِ النَّاسِ. Mostly the true God, and then the news is usually "one" (إِلَٰهُكُمْ إِلَٰهٌ وَاحِدٌ — وَاحِد is in its own arc). al-Zamakhsharī at 114:3: "Lord of people" and "King of people" can be said of others, but إِلَٰهِ النَّاسِ is God''s alone. The same frame can name a false god from the speaker''s side: the calf, "this is your god" (20:88, 20:97), and Pharaoh mocking "the god of Moses" (28:38, 40:37).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The verb takes two objects: the thing, and the god it is made into (Ibn ʿĀshūr). The Lisān, Lane and al-Rāghib define the word this way: whatever is worshipped is a god to its worshipper. Some even take their own desire as god (25:43, 45:23).' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:ittakhadha' AND boundary_note_en IS 'اتَّخَذَ takes two objects: the thing, and what it is turned into — إِلَٰهًا / آلِهَةً (Ibn ʿĀshūr at 25:43, p.35). This is the lexicons'' own definition of the word: whatever is taken as worshipped besides God is an إِلَٰه to the one who takes it (Lisān p.7028; Lane p.82: "accord. to him who takes it as such"; al-Rāghib p.82: they made إله a name for everything they worshipped). Most add مِن دُونِ اللَّهِ. Special cases: one''s own desire taken as god (25:43, 45:23); "make us a god" (7:138) and "shall I seek you a god other than God?" (7:140); Pharaoh''s threat "if you take a god other than me" (26:29).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «لَا» here denies every god of any kind, and «إِلَّا» gives the one exception: always God. The word "exists" is left unsaid, since the point is plain (Ibn ʿĀshūr on 2:163). Al-Zamakhsharī: it proves oneness by denying all others and affirming Him.' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:la_illa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'لا here wipes out the whole kind: not one god of any sort. إلّا then gives the one exception, and it is always God (هُوَ, أَنَا, أَنتَ, اللَّهُ; in 10:90 Pharaoh, drowning, says "except the One the Children of Israel believe in"). The word "exists" is left unsaid, because every hearer knows the point is to deny the thing itself (Ibn ʿĀshūr at 2:163, p.75). al-Zamakhsharī at 2:163: it fixes oneness by denying every other and affirming Him. al-Rāghib (p.82): الإله is the one worshipped.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'A proof from the impossible: more gods would mean ruin (21:22), or a reach for the Throne (17:42). In 21:22 «إِلَّا» means "other than" (al-Zamakhsharī). At 23:91 «لَوْ» is left out but meant, and «إِذًا» marks the answer (al-Zamakhsharī, Ibn ʿĀshūr).' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:law' AND boundary_note_en IS 'A proof from the impossible: suppose more gods, and heaven and earth would be ruined (21:22), or they would look for a way up to the Lord of the Throne (17:42). In 21:22 إِلَّا means "other than" — al-Zamakhsharī: "many gods besides the One who made them". 23:91 has no لَوْ but means it: al-Zamakhsharī says the condition is left out — "if there had been gods with Him" — and إِذًا marks the answer (so too Ibn ʿĀshūr, p.114). 21:99 turns it on the idols: had they been gods, they would not have gone into the Fire.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «مِنْ» after "not" adds only force: no god of any kind (al-Zamakhsharī, Ibn ʿĀshūr, al-Ālūsī). It opens the call of Noah, Hūd, Ṣāliḥ and Shuʿayb: «مَا لَكُم مِّنْ إِلَٰهٍ غَيْرُهُ» (7:59). Pharaoh turns it round: «مَا عَلِمْتُ لَكُم مِّنْ إِلَٰهٍ غَيْرِي» (28:38).' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:ma_min' AND boundary_note_en IS 'مِن after a "no" is extra in grammar but not in meaning: it makes the denial cover every god, like the لا of the last arc (al-Zamakhsharī at 3:62; Ibn ʿĀshūr at 3:62, p.267: it makes plain the whole kind is denied; al-Ālūsī: مِن زائدة للتأكيد). It is the opening call of Noah, Hūd, Ṣāliḥ and Shuʿayb: اعْبُدُوا اللَّهَ مَا لَكُم مِّنْ إِلَٰهٍ غَيْرُهُ. غَيْرُهُ was also read غَيْرِهِ and غَيْرَهُ — al-Zamakhsharī at 7:59: by the place of إِلَٰه, by its word, or as "except Him". Pharaoh turns the formula round in 28:38: مَا عَلِمْتُ لَكُم مِّنْ إِلَٰهٍ غَيْرِي.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The question expects "no": it comes after a list of what God alone does. Al-Zamakhsharī reads «أَإِلَٰهٌ مَعَ اللَّهِ» (27:60) as "is any partner joined to Him?" Ibn ʿĀshūr: the question is a denial, drawn from the proof just before it.' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:question' AND boundary_note_en IS 'A question that expects "no": after naming what God alone does, it asks who else could. al-Zamakhsharī at 27:60: أَإِلَٰهٌ مَعَ اللَّهِ means "is something else joined to Him as a partner?"; Ibn ʿĀshūr (p.11): the question denies, and it is the result of the proof just before it. With مَنْ (6:46, 28:71–72) it asks which god other than God would bring it back; with أَمْ (21:43, 52:43) "or do they have a god…?". al-Rāghib (p.82) quotes 21:43 for the plural آلِهَة.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Al-Zamakhsharī (on 16:51): without "one", it would prove He is a god, not that He is one. Ibn ʿĀshūr (on 2:163): to the Arabs a god was just "one worshipped", so they had many. In 38:5 the idolaters complain he made the gods one.' WHERE id = 'ARC:اله:ilah:wahid' AND boundary_note_en IS 'إِلَٰهٌ alone could just mean "a god"; adding وَاحِدٌ makes the number the point. al-Zamakhsharī at 16:51: if you said only إنما هو إله without واحد, it would sound as if you were proving He is a god, not that He is one. Ibn ʿĀshūr at 2:163 (p.75): in Arab speech الإله is just "the one worshipped", which is why they had many; the verse is aimed first at idolaters, who did not know a god can only be one. Mostly the news after إِلَٰهُكُمْ or إِنَّمَا هُوَ; in 38:5 it is the idolaters'' complaint: "has he made the gods one god?"';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle sets the target: «فِي» puts the blessing in a place, «عَلَى» on a person. The particle «عَلَى» occurs only once, at 37:113, of Ibrāhīm and Isḥāq.' WHERE id = 'ARC:برك:baraka:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The harf changes the target: في lands it in a PLACE, على on a PERSON. 37:113 is the only على, and its objects are Ibrahim and Ishaq.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It occurs once, of the land around al-Aqṣā. The good is neither in the place nor on a person: it surrounds it.' WHERE id = 'ARC:برك:baraka:hawla' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Once only, of the precincts of al-Aqsa. Neither in the place nor on a person but encircling it.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It is always something opened or sent upon: from sky and earth (7:96), upon Nūḥ (11:48), and upon the household (11:73).' WHERE id = 'ARC:برك:barakat' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Always of what is opened upon or comes upon: from sky and earth at 7:96, upon Nuh at 11:48, upon the household at 11:73.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'This is the only passive, at the burning bush. The one who blesses is not named, just as God is about to speak.' WHERE id = 'ARC:برك:burika:passive' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The only passive, at the burning bush. The agent is unnamed at the moment God is about to speak.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'This is the masculine form; the feminine has its own card with the same sense. They are separate words in the word list, but a learner should meet them together.' WHERE id = 'ARC:برك:mubarak:sifa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The masculine of the same participle. Same construction, different gender — one arc each because they are separate canonical lemmas, but the sense is identical and a learner should meet them together.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'All four describe a noun: a tree, a greeting, a patch of ground, a night. Only 28:30 is definite, but that changes the ending, not the construction.' WHERE id = 'ARC:برك:mubaraka:sifa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'All four are the same construction: the feminine participle qualifying a noun. 28:30 is definite, the other three indefinite — definiteness is inflection, not government, so it opens no second arc. What varies is WHAT is qualified: a tree, a greeting, a patch of ground, a night.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'All nine cases have God or His name as subject. Form VI here does not mean "to each other"; it raises the sense to the highest degree. It has no verbal noun, command or participle in use, which marks how narrow it is.' WHERE id = 'ARC:برك:tabaraka' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Nine attestations, every one with God or His name as subject. Form VI here is not reciprocal but elative, and it has no masdar, no imperative and no participle in use — the gap is itself the mark of its restriction.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'God is always the subject. Al-Rāghib: it is one event seen from two sides — the person turns to God, and God turns to him. Al-Jawharī adds a second sense, "God helped him turn", which fits 9:118.' WHERE id = 'ARC:توب:taba:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Here God is always the subject. One event, two names: the person تاب إلى God, and God تاب على him (al-Rāghib). al-Jawharī adds a second sense — God helped him to turn — which fits 9:118.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The sense is the same as with «إِلَى»; the goal is clear, so it is not named. Phrases that follow, like «مِن قَرِيبٍ» (4:17), say when, not where. The one who turns is always the person.' WHERE id = 'ARC:توب:taba:bare' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Same sense as with إلى; the goal is just not named, because it is clear. Phrases that often follow — مِن قَرِيبٍ, مِنۢ بَعۡدِ ذَٰلِكَ — say WHEN, not where; they are not the verb''s particle. The subject is always the person.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The one who turns is always the person, and God is where he turns. When God is the subject, the Qurʾān uses «عَلَى», never «إِلَى».' WHERE id = 'ARC:توب:taba:ila' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The one who turns is always the person, and God is where he turns to. When God is the subject, the Qurʾān never uses إلى — it uses على (next arc).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The phrase «قَابِلِ ٱلتَّوۡبِ» (40:3) is the noun form of «يَقۡبَلُ ٱلتَّوۡبَةَ» (42:25). Al-Zamakhsharī: it names a lasting quality of God, not one act. The Lisān: the word is a verbal noun, or the plural of "a repentance".' WHERE id = 'ARC:توب:tawb:idafa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'قَابِلِ ٱلتَّوۡبِ is the noun form of يَقۡبَلُ ٱلتَّوۡبَةَ (42:25). al-Zamakhsharī: it names a lasting quality of God, not one act at one time. التَّوۡب is the maṣdar, or a plural of تَوۡبَة (Lisān).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Here the word means God''s acceptance of the turning (al-Ālūsī). The particle «عَلَى» marks a duty God binds on Himself by His promise (al-Zamakhsharī), and «لِـ» names who it is for. Verse 4:18 denies it to those who wait until death.' WHERE id = 'ARC:توب:tawba:ala_lam' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Here التوبة means God''s acceptance of the turning (al-Ālūsī). على marks what is binding — by His own promise (al-Zamakhsharī); لِـ names who it is for. 4:18 denies it to those who wait until death.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «مِنَ» shows the turning starts from God, so it is His mercy, not the person''s repentance (Ibn ʿĀshūr). Al-Zamakhsharī: the fast is laid down as that mercy.' WHERE id = 'ARC:توب:tawba:min' AND boundary_note_en IS 'مِن shows the turning starts from God, so it means His acceptance and mercy, not the person''s repentance (Ibn ʿĀshūr). The fast is laid down as that mercy (al-Zamakhsharī).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The verbal noun adds no new sense. It makes room for «نَصُوحًا» (66:8): a turning with no plan to go back (Ibn ʿĀshūr).' WHERE id = 'ARC:توب:tawba:mutlaq' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The verb already says "turn back to God"; the maṣdar adds no new sense — it makes room for نَصُوحًا, a turning with no plan to go back (Ibn ʿĀshūr).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «عَنْ» is read two ways. Al-Ālūsī: it adds the sense of passing over the sins; al-Rāzī: it simply means "from". In 3:90 it is denied: their turning will not be received.' WHERE id = 'ARC:توب:tawba:qabul_an' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Two readings of عن: it brings in the sense of passing over the sins (al-Ālūsī), or it simply means مِن, "from" (al-Rāzī). In 3:90 the pair is denied: their turning will not be received.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «بِـ» gives the cause, after a verb of seizing, ruining or striking: «فَأَخَذَهُمُ ٱللَّهُ بِذُنُوبِهِمْ» (3:11). Al-Rāghib: a sin is named from the tail, because its outcome trails behind it. Sins one confesses or knows of (9:102, 17:17) belong elsewhere.' WHERE id = 'ARC:ذنب:dhanb:bi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'بِـ of cause after a verb of seizing, ruining or striking. Rāghib: the sin is called ذَنْب from the tail (ذَنَب), because its outcome trails behind it, and cites فَأَخَذَهُمُ ٱللَّهُ بِذُنُوبِهِمْ. Not here: اعْتَرَفُوا بِذُنُوبِهِمْ (9:102, 40:11, 67:11) and خَبِيرًا بِذُنُوبِ (17:17, 25:58) — there the بِـ belongs to the verb or adjective.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It occurs once (26:14). The particle «لِـ» names who holds the claim, «عَلَى» the one it lies on. Ibn ʿĀshūr: the "sin" means its result, their right to blood for the slain Copt; al-Ālūsī: a sin only in their eyes.' WHERE id = 'ARC:ذنب:dhanb:lahum_ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Only at 26:14. لـ names those who hold the claim, عَلَى the one it lies on. The tafsīr: the ''sin'' here is its consequence — the right to demand blood for the slain Copt (Ibn ʿĀshūr); a sin ''in their eyes'' (Ālūsī).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Lane, the Lisān and the Tāj read «وَٱشۡدُدۡ عَلَىٰ قُلُوبِهِمۡ» (10:88) as "seal their hearts"; al-Ālūsī: harden and seal them. Ibn ʿĀshūr alone reads "bring distress into their hearts". It is not Form II, "be hard on".' WHERE id = 'ARC:شدد:shadda:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The books read وَٱشۡدُدۡ عَلَىٰ قُلُوبِهِمۡ as اطبع على قلوبهم, "seal their hearts" (Lane, Lisān, Tāj; al-Ālūsī: make them hard and seal them). Ibn ʿĀshūr alone takes it from الشِّدَّة, hardship: "bring distress into their hearts". Not Form II شدّد عليه, "be hard on".';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The object is what grows strong (Mūsā''s back, his arm), and «بِـ» names the helper, Hārūn. The lexicons give "strengthen" and quote 20:31, but do not explain the «بِـ».' WHERE id = 'ARC:شدد:shadda:bi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The object is what grows strong (Mūsā''s back, his arm); بِـ names the helper (Hārūn). The books give "strengthen" and quote 20:31 but do not explain the بِـ.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It is literal in 47:4 (tie the bonds), of the body''s frame in 76:28, and of a kingdom in 38:20. The Lisān: to bind a kingdom tight is to make it strong.' WHERE id = 'ARC:شدد:shadda:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Literal in 47:4 (tie the bonds); of the body''s frame in 76:28; of a kingdom in 38:20 — the knot becomes strength (Lisān: شدّ الله ملكه = قوّاه).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «عَلَى» aims the hardness at someone (Lane: "hard toward his people"). The verse sets it against «رُحَمَآءُ بَيۡنَهُمۡ» (48:29): hard toward the enemy, gentle among themselves.' WHERE id = 'ARC:شدد:shadid:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'على aims the hardness at someone (Lane: شديد على قومه, "hard toward his people"). The verse sets it against رُحَمَآءُ بَيۡنَهُمۡ: hard toward the enemy, gentle among themselves.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The adjective describes God (or Jibrīl, in 53:5), but what is severe is the noun after it. The phrase «شَدِيدُ ٱلۡعِقَابِ» (3:11) means "whose punishment is severe" (al-Zamakhsharī). Ibn ʿĀshūr: the phrase only lightens the wording; the second noun is the real subject.' WHERE id = 'ARC:شدد:shadid:idafa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The adjective describes God (Jibrīl in 53:5), but what is severe is the noun after it: شَدِيدُ ٱلۡعِقَابِ = "whose punishment is severe" (al-Zamakhsharī). This iḍāfa only lightens the wording; the second noun is the real subject (Ibn ʿĀshūr).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'There are two readings. Al-Zajjāj (in the Lisān) and al-Rāzī: out of love of wealth he is tight-fisted. Al-Zamakhsharī: he is intense in his love of wealth, which al-Ālūsī finds more likely.' WHERE id = 'ARC:شدد:shadid:lam' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Two readings. (1) لِـ gives the reason and شديد = miserly: "out of love of wealth he is tight-fisted" (al-Zajjāj in Lisān; al-Rāzī). (2) لِـ = "in", شديد = strong: "he is intense in his love of wealth" (al-Zamakhsharī, as quoted by al-Ālūsī, who thinks it more likely).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Nothing follows the word. The thing named, such as a punishment, a force or a quake, is itself severe.' WHERE id = 'ARC:شدد:shadid:plain' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Nothing follows the word: the thing named — a punishment, a force, a quake — is itself severe.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'After "how bad is", the word names the end-place itself. Al-Ālūsī (on 2:126): what is blamed, the Fire, is often left unsaid. The Lisān gives "where is your home?" for this word.' WHERE id = 'ARC:صير:masir:bisa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'After بئس / ساءت the word is a noun of place — the end-place itself; what is blamed (the Fire) is often left unsaid (al-Ālūsī on 2:126). Lisān: أين مصيركم = "where is your home?"';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Here the word is a verbal noun: the going back, by death and raising (al-Ālūsī on 2:285). Lane: it implies a change of state, unlike the plain word for "return". In 12 of 13 cases "to God" comes first: to Him alone.' WHERE id = 'ARC:صير:masir:ila' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Here المصير is a verbal noun: the going back, by death and raising (al-Ālūsī on 2:285). Lane: مصير implies a change from the earlier state, which مَرۡجِع does not. In 12 of 13 cases إلى + God comes first ("to Him alone"); 14:30 keeps normal order, with the Fire as the end.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'This is the praised twin of «بِئۡسَ ٱلۡمَصِيرُ» (2:126). Paradise is both the reward and the place (al-Zamakhsharī). The Lisān: a good dwelling is also called by this word.' WHERE id = 'ARC:صير:masir:praise' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The praised twin of بِئۡسَ ٱلۡمَصِيرُ: Paradise is both the reward and the place (al-Zamakhsharī: He praised the reward and its place). Lisān: a good dwelling is called مَصِير.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The phrase «إِلَى ٱللَّهِ» (42:53) comes first to limit it: to God and no one else (al-Ālūsī, Ibn ʿĀshūr).' WHERE id = 'ARC:صير:sara:ila' AND boundary_note_en IS 'إِلَى ٱللَّهِ is put first for restriction: to God, not to anyone else (al-Ālūsī; Ibn ʿĀshūr). QAC stores this lemma as the inflected تَصِيرُ.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'In all three the subject is time: a span, a period, a life. The particle «عَلَى» puts the people under it. With a person as subject it means "did him a favour" (al-Jawharī, Lane), but that sense is not in the Qurʾān.' WHERE id = 'ARC:طول:tala:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'In all three the subject is time — الأمد, العهد, العمر — and على puts the people under it. Keep apart the other طال عليه, with a person as subject: "he did him a favour" (al-Jawharī; Lane). That sense does not occur in the Qurʾān.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Form VI adds a long, drawn-out stretching. With a person as subject it means "he lorded it over him" (the Lisān, the Miṣbāḥ). Here the subject is life, so the sense is time (Lane on 28:45).' WHERE id = 'ARC:طول:tatawala:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Form VI adds a long, drawn-out stretching. With a person as subject, تَطَاوَلَ عَلَيۡهِ means "he lorded it over him" (Lisān; al-Miṣbāḥ). Here the subject is ٱلۡعُمُر, so the sense is time, as with طَالَ عَلَيۡهِمُ (Lane on 28:45).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The word before it means "owner of". Of God (40:3) it is bounty and increase (al-Zamakhsharī). Of men (9:86) it is people of wealth and means (al-Ṭabarī).' WHERE id = 'ARC:طول:tawl:dhu' AND boundary_note_en IS 'ذُو / أُولُو mean "owner(s) of". Of God (40:3): bounty and power — al-Zamakhsharī: الفضل والزيادة. Of men (9:86): people of means — al-Ṭabarī: ذوو الغنى والمال.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'As the object of "is able", the word is the means itself, and "to marry" says what it is for. Al-Zajjāj (in the Lisān): the power to pay the dower. Ibn ʿĀshūr: ability.' WHERE id = 'ARC:طول:tawl:object' AND boundary_note_en IS 'As the object of يَسۡتَطِعۡ the word is the means itself, and أَن يَنكِحَ says what it is for (al-Zajjāj in Lisān: power to pay the dower; Ibn ʿĀshūr: القدرة).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'In «أَعَزُّ نَفَرًا» (18:34) it means more helpers and followers (al-Zamakhsharī). In 63:8 the hypocrite calls himself the mightiest and is answered at once: «وَلِلَّهِ الْعِزَّةُ» (63:8). With «عَلَى» (11:92), see the "dear to" sense.' WHERE id = 'ARC:عزز:aazz:tafdil' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Without على. 18:34 أَعَزُّ نَفَرًا (Zamakhsharī: more helpers and retinue). 63:8 الْأَعَزُّ is the hypocrite''s name for himself, answered at once: وَلِلَّهِ الْعِزَّةُ. With على (11:92) see the "dear to" arc.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Form IV passes the state on to someone. It is paired with «تُذِلُّ» (3:26), "bring low".' WHERE id = 'ARC:عزز:aazza:fil' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Form IV passes the state on to someone; in 3:26 paired with تُذِلُّ, bring low.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'One particle, three senses. Which one applies depends on the subject: a matter, a person, or a people facing the enemy.' WHERE id = 'ARC:عزز:aziz:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'One particle, several senses, told apart by what it governs: (1) hardness → a matter that is hard on someone, that weighs on him; (2) weight → a person who counts with you, too dear to harm; (3) hardness → hard against the enemy, never giving way.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'No particle follows. It stands alone or, most often, beside a second name such as Wise, Merciful, Knowing or Forgiving, so might is never shown without what guides it.' WHERE id = 'ARC:عزز:aziz:allah' AND boundary_note_en IS 'No harf. It stands alone or, most often, paired with a second name — wise, merciful, knowing, forgiving — so might is never shown without what guides it. Rāghib: العزيز is "who overcomes and is not overcome".';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Said of people it names rank, like the powerful men of a town (27:34). Al-Zamakhsharī (on 12:30): in Arab speech it means the king. In 44:49 it is mockery: the man who called himself mighty is told so in Hell (Ibn ʿĀshūr).' WHERE id = 'ARC:عزز:aziz:insan' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Said of people it names rank, not God''s might. Zamakhsharī 12:30: العزيز = the king, in the Arabs'' tongue. 27:34: the powerful of a town. 44:49 is mockery — the man who called himself mighty and noble is told so in Hell (Ibn ʿĀshūr: meant as its opposite).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The object, the two messengers, is left out; al-Zamakhsharī says this is because the point is the one added. It was also read «عَزَزْنَا» (36:14), "We overpowered them with a third".' WHERE id = 'ARC:عزز:azzaza:bi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The object (the two messengers) is left out; Zamakhsharī: because the point is the one added. Also read عَزَزْنَا, "We overpowered them by a third".';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «بِـ» carries what the payback is made of: "the like of" the wrong. In «عُوقِبۡتُم بِهِۦ» (16:126) the first wrong is called "punishment" only to match the wording (al-Zamakhsharī, Lane).' WHERE id = 'ARC:عقب:aqaba:bi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'بِـ carries what the payback is made of — بِمِثۡلِ, "the like of". In عُوقِبۡتُم بِهِۦ the first wrong is also called "punishment" only to match the wording (al-Zamakhsharī: المزاوجة; Lane).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'This is not punishment. It comes from taking turns, like two riders sharing one mount: "then your turn came" (al-Zamakhsharī, al-Ālūsī). Masrūq and al-Zajjāj (in the Lisān and Lane): "then you took spoils".' WHERE id = 'ARC:عقب:aqaba:turn' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Not punishment. From عُقۡبَة, a turn (two riders sharing one mount): "then your turn came" (al-Zamakhsharī, al-Ālūsī). Others: "then you took spoils" (Masrūq, al-Zajjāj — in Lisān and Lane).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The word always comes second, after "severe in" (14 times), "swift in" (6:165, 7:167) or "owner of" (41:43). Al-Rāghib: this word is kept for punishment. Forgiveness is often named in the same breath.' WHERE id = 'ARC:عقب:iqab:idafa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'العِقَاب is always the second noun: after شَدِيدُ (14×), سَرِيعُ (6:165, 7:167) or ذُو (41:43). al-Rāghib: عقاب is kept for punishment. Often forgiveness is named in the same breath (6:165, 7:167, 41:43).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The final letter for "my" is dropped in writing, but it still means "My punishment" (Ibn ʿĀshūr). The setting is a respite first, then the seizing, so the root''s sense of "after" shows.' WHERE id = 'ARC:عقب:iqab:mutakallim' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The final ي is dropped; عِقَابِ = عِقَابِي (Ibn ʿĀshūr). The setting is a respite first, then the seizing — the root''s "after" shows.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It is most often paired with another name, like Hearing or Wise. Al-Rāzī (on 2:32): in full it means knowing all that can be known, which is God''s alone. Of people it is limited: "over every knower is one who knows more" (12:76, al-Rāghib).' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:alim:bare' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Most often paired with another name (سَمِيعٌ عَلِيمٌ, عَلِيمٌ حَكِيمٌ, وَاسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ). Al-Rāzī (2:32): the full force of عَلِيم means taking in all that can be known, which is God''s alone. Said of a person, it is measured and limited: a skilled sorcerer, Yūsuf over the stores, the boy promised to Ibrāhīm, and 12:76 ''over every knower is one who knows more'' (Rāghib). The plural عُلَمَاء at 26:197 and 35:28 is filed under this lemma but is a noun, ''people of knowledge'', and is left out.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The «بِـ» phrase may come after the word or before it; that is only word order. Where «بِـ» belongs to an earlier verb, as in «وَكَفَىٰ بِٱللَّهِ عَلِيمًا» (4:70), it is not counted here.' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:alim:bi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The بِـ phrase may come after (عَلِيمٌ بِذَاتِ ٱلصُّدُورِ) or before (بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيمٌ); fronting is word order, not a new government. Not here: 7:112, 10:79, 26:37 بِكُلِّ سَاحِرٍ عَلِيمٍ and 15:53, 51:28 بِغُلَٰمٍ عَلِيمٍ — that بِـ belongs to the verb before it; 4:70 وَكَفَىٰ بِٱللَّهِ عَلِيمًا — the بِـ belongs to كَفَىٰ.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'There is no object at all, as in «وَأَنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ» (2:22). Ibn ʿĀshūr (on 39:9): the verb stands alone, meaning "those who have knowledge". In «مَا لَا تَعْلَمُونَ» (2:30) the relative word is the object, so it belongs to the direct card.' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:alima:absolute' AND boundary_note_en IS 'No object at all, and none fronted: لَا يَعْلَمُونَ, إِن كُنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ, وَأَنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ. Ibn ʿĀshūr (39:9): the verb is treated as intransitive — ''those who have knowledge''. When مَا لَا تَعْلَمُونَ has a relative مَا, that مَا is the object, and the use belongs to the direct arc.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The verb takes a whole statement, not a thing: «أَنَّ» fills the place of both objects. When a question word or a negation cuts the verb off from its object, the sense is the same, so those cases are kept here.' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:alima:anna' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The verb takes a whole statement, not a thing: أَنَّ (or light أَنْ) fills the place of the two objects. Where a question word, a مَا of denial or a لام cuts the verb off from a plain object (taʿlīq), the sense is the same — knowing how things stand — so those are kept here as a branch.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Only here does the verb take «بِـ». The commentaries are silent on it. The lexicons say «بِـ» comes in when the verb means "become aware" (the Miṣbāḥ, the Tāj) or "take it all in" (the Tāj).' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:alima:bi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Only here does the verb عَلِمَ itself take بِـ. The tafsīr is silent on this بِـ; the lexicons say بِـ comes in when عَلِمَ carries the sense of شَعَرَ ''become aware'' (Miṣbāḥ, Tāj), or of taking it all in, الإحاطة (Tāj) — the same gloss the government row gives عَلِيمٌ بِـ.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'With one object the verb means "know it" or "know who it is", as the lexicons say. The object may be a noun, a pronoun or a relative word. Only in «مِنَ ٱلْمُصْلِحِ» (2:220) do the commentaries read it as "tell apart".' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:alima:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'One object, and the verb means ''know it / know who it is'' (the books: عَلِمَ = عَرَفَ here). The object may be a noun, a pronoun, or a relative مَا / مَنْ / الَّذِينَ — also when the مَا comes first (مَا لَا تَعْلَمُونَ). In 2:143 and 34:21 the مِنْ belongs to مِمَّنْ, not to the verb (as the government row says); only at 2:220 does the tafsīr hang مِنَ ٱلْمُصْلِحِ on the verb, read as ''tell apart''.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'This is the only place with two objects: a person and a description. Al-Rāghib and the Tāj set it beside 8:60 to show two kinds of knowing. The commentaries: it is a firm judgement from signs, since «ٱللَّهُ أَعْلَمُ بِإِيمَٰنِهِنَّ» (60:10).' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:alima:two_obj' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The only place where عَلِمَ takes a person and a description as two objects. Rāghib (and the Tāj after him) pair it with 8:60 to show the two kinds of عِلْم. The tafsīr says the ''knowing'' here is a strong judgement from oaths and signs, since only God knows the heart (ٱللَّهُ أَعْلَمُ بِإِيمَٰنِهِنَّ).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'This is the only case with «بِـ», meaning "inform" (al-Ālūsī; the Miṣbāḥ has the same pattern). The person comes directly and the matter by «بِـ». Said to people who would "inform" God of their faith, it mocks them.' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:allama:bi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The only عَلَّمَ with بِـ. Ālūsī: it comes from عَلِمْتُ بِهِ, so the doubled form takes the person directly and the matter by بِـ — ''inform''. The Miṣbāḥ gives the same pattern for أَعْلَمْتُهُ بِهِ. Said to people who would ''inform'' God of their faith, it mocks them.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The meaning is the same; only the stress changes. At 55:2 the learner is left out, so the gift is the teaching itself (Abū Ḥayyān). At 96:4 only the means is left: the pen.' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:allama:one_side' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Not a different meaning of ''teach'' — a different stress. At 55:2 the learner is dropped, so the gift is the teaching itself, not who got it (Abū Ḥayyān). At 96:4 both sides are dropped and only the means remains: the pen. 3:79 counts here (تُعَلِّمُونَ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ, no learner).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Both sides are named: the learner, then the lesson. Al-Rāghib (in the Tāj): this teaching is repeated until it settles, unlike a quick telling.' WHERE id = 'ARC:علم:allama:two_obj' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Both sides named: the learner, then the lesson. The lesson may be a relative مَا (مَا لَمْ تَعْلَمُوا). Rāghib (in the Tāj): تَعْلِيم is teaching by repetition until it settles, unlike إِعْلَام, a quick telling.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Nothing follows the verb. In three of the four, people let a wrong pass; al-Zamakhsharī (in Abū Ḥayyān) reads «هُمْ يَغْفِرُونَ» (42:37) as "they are the ones who forgive, even in anger". At 23:118 it is a prayer to God.' WHERE id = 'ARC:غفر:ghafara:absolute' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Nothing follows the verb. Three of the four are people letting a wrong pass (42:37, 42:43, 64:14); Zamakhsharī (in Abū Ḥayyān 42:37) reads هُمْ يَغْفِرُونَ as ''they are the ones marked by forgiving, even in anger''. 23:118 رَبِّ ٱغْفِرْ وَٱرْحَمْ is a prayer to God with both objects left open.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The verb reaches only the sin; no person is named, and the one who forgives is always God. In 4:48 the first verb belongs here, while the second, with «لِمَن يَشَآءُ» (4:48), belongs to the «لِـ» card.' WHERE id = 'ARC:غفر:ghafara:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The verb reaches only the sin; no person is named. Always God. At 4:48 and 4:116 the first يَغْفِرُ takes أَن يُشْرَكَ بِهِ as object (this arc); the second adds لِمَن يَشَآءُ and belongs to the لـ arc.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «لِـ» names the one who gains. The sin may be named after it or left out; the sense is the same. Al-Zamakhsharī: "some of your sins" (14:10) is said to unbelievers, "your sins" to believers.' WHERE id = 'ARC:غفر:ghafara:li' AND boundary_note_en IS 'لـ names the person who gains (the government row: lām of ''for''). The sin may be named after it (لَنَا ذُنُوبَنَا) or left out (لِمَن يَشَآءُ) — the sense is the same, so it is one arc with a branch. With مِنْ ذُنُوبِكُمْ (14:10, 46:31, 71:4) Zamakhsharī notes this form is said to unbelievers, plain ذُنُوبَكُمْ to believers.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'In «غَافِرِ ٱلذَّنۢبِ» (40:3) the word is joined to what it covers. Al-Ālūsī: this names a lasting trait, not one act. That is why it can be a fixed title of God (Ibn ʿĀshūr).' WHERE id = 'ARC:غفر:ghafir:idafa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The participle is joined to its object (غَافِرِ ٱلذَّنۢبِ) instead of taking it by a verb. Ālūsī: these participles are not about one act at one time but a fixed, lasting trait — which is why the iḍāfa makes them true epithets of God (Ibn ʿĀshūr: definite epithets). The only other غَافِر, 7:155 خَيْرُ ٱلْغَٰفِرِينَ, is plural with nothing covered named, and stands outside this arc.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'With no object, the asking is something they keep doing. Ibn ʿĀshūr (on 8:33): what counts is turning away from associating partners with God, not words alone.' WHERE id = 'ARC:غفر:istaghfara:absolute' AND boundary_note_en IS 'No object and no لـ: the asking is described as something they keep doing. Ibn ʿĀshūr (8:33): what counts is turning from shirk, not words alone.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The object is the one asked, always God. Al-Rāghib (on 71:10): they were told to ask by word and by deed. The sin is never the object in the Qurʾān, though the lexicons allow it.' WHERE id = 'ARC:غفر:istaghfara:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The object is the one asked — always God (رَبَّكُمْ, ٱللَّهَ, or a pronoun for Him). Rāghib on 71:10: they were told to ask by tongue and by deed. The sin is never the object here in the Qurʾān, though the books allow اسْتَغْفَرَ ٱللَّهَ ذَنْبَهُ.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «لِـ» can name the sin itself, meaning "from" (the Ṣiḥāḥ, the Lisān). Or it names the person prayed for, never the one praying. Verse 47:19 has both: one''s own fault, then the believers (Ibn ʿĀshūr).' WHERE id = 'ARC:غفر:istaghfara:li' AND boundary_note_en IS 'لـ here points to the sin itself. The Ṣiḥāḥ and Lisān: اسْتَغْفَرَ لِذَنْبِهِ and مِنْ ذَنْبِهِ mean the same. Ibn ʿĀshūr (47:19): the لـ of لِذَنۢبِكَ is ''lām of specifying'' standing for a second object; the لـ of وَلِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ that follows is a different one — see the next arc. لـ names the person prayed for, never the one praying. 47:19 holds both kinds in one verb: لِذَنۢبِكَ (own fault) and وَلِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ (others: Ibn ʿĀshūr — lām of ''for the sake of'', their sins understood); it is counted in both arcs. Two lāms, one arc: لِذَنْبِكَ names the sin forgiven, لَهُمْ the people prayed for (Ibn ʿĀshūr on 47:19, where both stand).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It occurs once, beside «غَافِرِ ٱلذَّنۢبِ» (40:3). The "and" between the two names gives both mercies: the turning is taken and the sin is covered (al-Zamakhsharī). The second word is a verbal noun (Ibn ʿĀshūr) or a group plural (al-Rāzī).' WHERE id = 'ARC:قبل:qabil:idafa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Once, beside غَافِرِ ٱلذَّنۢبِ. التَّوْب is a masdar ''turning back'' (Ibn ʿĀshūr) or a collective of تَوْبَة (al-Rāzī). The و between the two names gives the repenter both mercies together: his turning is taken, and his sin is covered (Zamakhsharī, in Ālūsī and Ibn ʿĀshūr).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'God is the subject and repentance the object. The Lisān treats «مِنْ» and «عَنْ» alike, but al-Ālūsī says «عَنْ» adds passing over and pardoning. Abū Ḥayyān: «عَنْ» means moving away, stronger than «مِنْ».' WHERE id = 'ARC:قبل:qabila:an' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Both with التَّوْبَة as object and God as subject. The Lisān treats مِنْ and عَنْ alike here (as the government row notes); the tafsīr gives عَنْ its own weight: Ālūsī — the verb carries ''passing over and pardoning''; Abū Ḥayyān — عَنْ is ''moving away'', stronger than مِنْ.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'No particle is used. The owner is named inside the object, as in "their repentance", or by "for them", which al-Ālūsī reads as "their testimony". Both cases are denials.' WHERE id = 'ARC:قبل:qabila:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'No مِنْ or عَنْ: the owner is named inside the object phrase (تَوْبَتُهُمْ), or by لَهُمْ next to the noun, which Ālūsī reads as ''their testimony''. The verb says only whether the thing counts — both are denials.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «مِنْ» names the giver. All five are denied and passive: ransom, intercession, spending, and a religion other than Islam will not be taken from him.' WHERE id = 'ARC:قبل:qabila:min' AND boundary_note_en IS 'مِنْ names the giver. All five are denied and passive — ransom, intercession, spending, a religion other than Islam: ''it will not be taken from him''. At 3:85 the object is understood (the religion he sought).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The Lisān treats «مِنْ» and «عَنْ» alike here. Ibn ʿĀshūr reads «عَنْ» as "on behalf of", since the man also prays for his parents and children. It also matches the next verb: «وَنَتَجَاوَزُ عَن سَيِّـَٔاتِهِمْ» (46:16).' WHERE id = 'ARC:قبل:taqabbala:an' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Once. The Lisān has the line يَقْبَلُ الأَعمال مِنْ عِبَادِهِ وَعَنْهُمْ — مِنْ and عَنْ alike. Ibn ʿĀshūr says مِنْ would be expected and reads عَنْ as ''on behalf of'', since the man also prays for his parents and children. It also mirrors the next verb, وَنَتَجَاوَزُ عَن سَيِّـَٔاتِهِمْ.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The thing offered is the object, and no giver is named. At 3:37 the offering is Maryam herself; al-Rāghib: "He accepted her", or "He took charge of her".' WHERE id = 'ARC:قبل:taqabbala:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Now the thing offered is the object and no giver is named. At 3:37 the ''offering'' is Maryam herself: Rāghib — ''He accepted her'', or ''He took charge of her''.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Only the giver is named, by «مِنْ»; the offering never is. Al-Rāghib: this is acceptance that brings reward, and not every act of worship is accepted (5:27).' WHERE id = 'ARC:قبل:taqabbala:min' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The offering is never named after the verb — only the giver, by مِنْ. Rāghib: تَقَبُّل is acceptance that brings reward, and ''not every act of worship is accepted'' (5:27).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «عَلَى» lays the writing on the person as a weight. Most cases are law, like fasting, fighting and retaliation; 3:154 and 59:3 are fate (al-Rāghib). In 6:12 and 6:54 God binds Himself to mercy.' WHERE id = 'ARC:كتب:kataba:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'على puts the writing over the person as a weight. Most are law (fasting, fighting, retaliation). 3:154 and 59:3 are fate (death, exile — Rāghib: القضاء الممضى). In 6:12 and 6:54 God writes mercy "on Himself": He binds Himself to it.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It is real writing in 2:79 and 2:282 (a debt). Elsewhere God or His messengers record what people say and do, to be repaid (al-Rāghib). In 52:41 and 68:47 he reads it as a claim to sure knowledge.' WHERE id = 'ARC:كتب:kataba:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'No harf: the verb reaches only the thing written. Real writing in 2:79 and 2:282 (a debt). In 3:181, 19:79, 36:12 and others God or His messengers record what people say and do — Rāghib: fixed for him and repaid. 52:41 and 68:47 (فَهُمْ يَكْتُبُونَ) Rāghib reads as a claim to sure knowledge.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Al-Rāghib (on 58:22): a heart written in is the opposite of the heart "left blank" in 18:28.' WHERE id = 'ARC:كتب:kataba:fi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Rāghib on 58:22: hearts written in are the opposite of the heart "left blank" (أَغْفَلْنَا) in 18:28.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Al-Rāghib (on 9:51): it says "for us", not "against us", so whatever befalls us is grace. At 5:21 it is "for you" because entering the land is good for them. At 9:120–121 it credits a deed to their account.' WHERE id = 'ARC:كتب:kataba:lam' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Rāghib on 9:51: "for us", not "against us" — whatever befalls us we count as grace; on 5:21: لَكُمْ not عَلَيْكُمْ, because entering the land would do them good. In 9:120–121 the لـ credits a deed to their account.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «مَعَ» is an adverb of place, not a preposition. Al-Rāghib (on 3:53): "place us in their company".' WHERE id = 'ARC:كتب:kataba:maa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'مَعَ is a ẓarf, not a ḥarf jarr. Rāghib on 3:53: "place us in their company".';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'What is written is a sworn sentence: «لَأَغْلِبَنَّ» (58:21). Al-Ālūsī: here the verb works like an oath. Al-Rāghib: a thing is willed, then said, then written, so writing is the firmest step.' WHERE id = 'ARC:كتب:kataba:qasam' AND boundary_note_en IS 'No على or لـ: what is written is a sworn sentence (لَأَغْلِبَنَّ). al-Ālūsī: كَتَبَ here "runs like an oath". Rāghib: a thing is willed, then said, then written — writing is the last and firmest step.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Here the word means a duty, not a book: with «عَلَى» it keeps its verb sense. Al-Zamakhsharī on «كِتَابَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ» (4:24): "God wrote it on you". At 4:103, prayer is written on the believers at set times.' WHERE id = 'ARC:كتب:kitab:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Not "a book": with على the noun keeps its maṣdar force. 4:24 كِتَابَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ — Zamakhsharī: an emphatic maṣdar, "God wrote it on you". 4:103: prayer is a كِتَاب on the believers, bound to set times.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «لِـ» names whose term it is: every term, every town. The same "written term" also appears in «كِتَابًا مُّؤَجَّلًا» (3:145) and «حَتَّىٰ يَبْلُغَ الْكِتَابُ أَجَلَهُ» (2:235), which are not counted here.' WHERE id = 'ARC:كتب:kitab:lam' AND boundary_note_en IS 'لـ names whose term it is (every term, every town). The same "written term" shows in other frames — 3:145 كِتَابًا مُّؤَجَّلًا, 2:235 حَتَّىٰ يَبْلُغَ الْكِتَابُ أَجَلَهُ — not counted here.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The verb reaches only the trouble. A person may be named, as in "what was on him" (21:84), but only inside the object, never as the verb''s target. That is what sets this card apart from the next.' WHERE id = 'ARC:كشف:verb:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The verb reaches only the trouble. A person may still be NAMED — 21:84 ma bihi, 23:75 ma bihim — but inside the object phrase, where the verb never touches him. That, not the absence of a person, is what separates this arc from the next.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «عَلَى» looks at where it comes from, above; «إِلَى» at where it ends. Al-Zamakhsharī and al-Rāzī (on 3:84): revelation is both, so both are used. Al-Rāghib''s finer rule, «عَلَى» for the prophet and «إِلَى» for his people, is doubted by al-Ālūsī.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:anzala:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'على looks at where it comes from: above. إلى (next arc) looks at where it ends: with someone. Zamakhsharī and al-Rāzī on 3:84: revelation is both, so the Qurʾān uses one, then the other (3:84 عَلَيْنَا, 2:136 إِلَيْنَا). Rāghib''s finer rule — على for what reaches a prophet directly, إلى for what reaches the community through him — is reported by Abū Ḥayyān and al-Ālūsī; al-Ālūsī doubts it (3:72 has على of the believers).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'With no receiver named, "what God sent down" becomes the rule to judge by (5:44–49). Of iron, troops or angels (57:25, 9:26, 23:24) it means made and given from above. Al-Rāghib: sending down a thing, or its causes.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:anzala:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'No receiver is named, so the sentence rests on the thing and the One who sent it: "what God sent down" becomes the rule to judge by and follow (5:44–49, 2:170). With iron, troops or angels (57:25, 9:26, 23:24) the books read it as made and given from above — Rāghib: sending down a thing, or its causes.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'One particle, two senses: «فِي» marks either the time it was sent or the thing it was placed inside.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:anzala:fi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'One particle, several senses, told apart by what it governs: (1) send down → at one set time, all at once; (2) send down → and set it inside something: a heart, a book.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «لِـ» names who benefits, not where it lands. Al-Zamakhsharī (on 39:6): decreed and shared out for you; Ibn ʿĀshūr: a mark of favour. It is used of cattle (39:6), provision (10:59) and rain (27:60).' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:anzala:lam' AND boundary_note_en IS 'لـ names who benefits, not where it lands. Zamakhsharī on 39:6: أَنزَلَ لَكُم = decreed and shared out for you, since God''s decrees are said to come down; Ibn ʿĀshūr: لَكُم marks a favour. Cattle (39:6), provision (10:59), rain (27:60).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «مَعَ» is an adverb of place, not a preposition, and names no receiver. The book is sent as the messengers'' companion; al-Ālūsī (on 2:213): "joined to the prophets".' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:anzala:maa' AND boundary_note_en IS 'مَعَ is a ẓarf, not a ḥarf jarr, and names no receiver: the book is sent as the messengers'' companion (al-Ālūsī on 2:213: a ḥāl of the book — "joined to the prophets").';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Two senses, told apart by what follows «مِنْ»: the sky, or a high place someone is brought down from.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:anzala:min' AND boundary_note_en IS 'One particle, several senses, told apart by what it governs: (1) send down from the sky → as rain on the ground; (2) bring someone down → off the high place that kept him safe.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The object is a person and the next word is the lodging. It is the old sense of stopping at a place, done to someone else (Lane: "he lodged him, made him his guest").' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:anzala:munzal' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The object is a person and the next word is the lodging: the old sense of نزول, stopping at a place, made to happen to someone (Lane: أَنْزَلَهُ = he lodged him, made him his guest).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The particle «بِـ» lets the verb take a thing, so "came down with it" means "brought it down" (al-Zamakhsharī). The carrier, Jibrīl, is the subject. In «وَبِالْحَقِّ نَزَلَ» (17:105) the «بِـ» only goes along with the descent.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:nazala:bi_haml' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The bāʾ makes the verb reach a thing (Zamakhsharī: للتعدية): نَزَلَ بِهِ = أَنْزَلَهُ, with the carrier (Jibrīl) as subject. In 17:105 وَبِالْحَقِّ نَزَلَ the bāʾ only goes along with the descent.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Here «بِـ» names where it lands, not a thing carried. It is said of the promised punishment; al-Ālūsī pictures an army falling on people in their homes.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:nazala:bi_makan' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Here بـ names where it lands, not a thing carried. Said of the promised punishment; al-Ālūsī: pictured as an army falling on people in their homes.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'There is no object and nothing carried: «مِن» only marks the start (34:2, 57:4), and "with the truth" only describes what came (17:105, 57:16). When «بِـ» carries a thing down, see the next card.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:nazala:lazim' AND boundary_note_en IS 'No object and nothing carried. In 34:2 and 57:4 مِن only marks where the fall starts (the sky). In 17:105 بِالْحَقِّ and 57:16 مِنَ الْحَقِّ only describe what came down. When بـ carries a thing down, see the next arc.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Al-Zamakhsharī (on 3:3): this form is for the Qurʾān, sent in parts, unlike the Torah and Gospel, sent whole. But 25:32 uses it for "all at once", and Abū Ḥayyān calls the two forms synonyms. So "piece by piece" is usual, not fixed (the Tāj).' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:nazzala:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Zamakhsharī (3:3): نَزَّلَ for the Qurʾān because it came in parts, أَنْزَلَ for the Torah and Gospel which came whole. But at 25:32 the deniers say نُزِّلَ … جُمْلَةً وَاحِدَةً, and Zamakhsharī must read نزّل there as plain أنزل; Abū Ḥayyān holds the doubled form is just a synonym of the hamza form. So "piece by piece" is the usual shade, not a fixed one (Tāj: the difference is "mostly").';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'All seven are denied. The particle «بِـ» ties the proof to the thing claimed (al-Zamakhsharī on 12:40), and the denial strikes both the sending and the proof (al-Ālūsī on 7:33). The lexicons give this «بِـ» only for Form I.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:nazzala:bi_sultan' AND boundary_note_en IS 'All seven are negated (مَا لَمْ يُنَزِّلْ / مَا أَنزَلَ). The bāʾ ties the proof to the thing claimed (Zamakhsharī 12:40: بِهَا = for naming them). al-Ālūsī 7:33: the denial strikes both the sending and the proof. From tafsīr: the lexicons have نزل بـ only for Form I, "brought down".';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Al-Rāghib (on 47:20): the believers ask for a sūra sent bit by bit, and when one is sent at once, the doubters shrink. Both forms stand in one verse. With "in measure" (15:21, 42:27) the sending is measured out.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:nazzala:direct' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Rāghib on 47:20: the believers ask that a sūra be نُزِّلَت (sent bit by bit), and when one is أُنزِلَت (sent at once) the doubters shrink — both forms in one verse. With بِقَدَرٍ (15:21, 42:27) the sending is measured out.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It is the same split as with Form IV: «إِلَى» looks at the end point, the people reached.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:nazzala:ila' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Same split as أنزل على / إلى: إلى looks at the end point, the people reached.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'It is the same picture as with Form IV. In 24:43 it sends down hail from mountains of cloud.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:nazzala:min' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Same picture as أَنْزَلَ مِنَ السَّمَاءِ; 24:43 sends down hail from mountains of cloud.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The verb''s own verbal noun follows it. Al-Zamakhsharī: "in step with events" (17:106) and "in parts, spread out" (76:23); al-Ālūsī: over some twenty-three years. In 25:25 it is said of the angels on the Last Day.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:nazzala:tanzilan' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The verb''s own maṣdar follows it. Zamakhsharī: 17:106 "in step with events", 76:23 "in parts, spread out"; al-Ālūsī 76:23: over some twenty-three years. In 25:25 it is said of the angels on the Last Day.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Form V is coming down done slowly (the Lisān). The particle «عَلَى» names who receives the visits: angels come to the steadfast (41:30), devils to every liar (26:221–222).' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:tanazzala:ala' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Form V is نزول done slowly (Lisān: النزول في مهلة). على names who receives the visits: angels on the steadfast (41:30), devils on every liar (26:221–222).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'Al-Rāghib: both forms are used of an angel bringing something, never of God, but for lies and devils only this form is used. Verse 26:210 denies that devils brought the Qurʾān down.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:tanazzala:bi' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Rāghib: you say نَزَلَ الملَكُ بكذا and تَنَزَّلَ, never of God; and for lies and what comes from devils only تَنَزَّلَ is used. 26:210 denies that devils brought the Qurʾān down.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'No receiver is named. What is named is the time (97:4), the space (65:12), or the command behind it: «بِأَمْرِ رَبِّكَ» (19:64).' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:tanazzala:lazim' AND boundary_note_en IS 'No receiver is named. What is named is the time (97:4 فِيهَا), the space (65:12 بَيْنَهُنَّ) or the order behind it (19:64 بِأَمْرِ رَبِّكَ).';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The word is joined to the thing sent, the Book, and «مِن» names the Sender. Al-Zamakhsharī (on 39:1): the «مِن» phrase belongs to "sending down". In 32:2 "no doubt in it" stands between them.' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:tanzil:idafa_kitab' AND boundary_note_en IS 'The iḍāfa goes to the thing sent (the Book); مِن then names the Sender (Zamakhsharī 39:1: the jārr belongs to التنزيل, "as you say: it came down from God"). In 32:2 لَا رَيْبَ فِيهِ stands between them.';
UPDATE ar_ling_lemma_arc SET boundary_note_en = 'The Book is not named; the Sender is, after «مِن» (20:4) or as the second word (26:192). So the verbal noun stands for the thing sent (al-Zamakhsharī on 26:192, al-Ālūsī on 36:5).' WHERE id = 'ARC:نزل:tanzil:munazzal' AND boundary_note_en IS 'Here the Book is not named; the Sender is — after مِن (20:4, 41:2, 41:42, 56:80, 69:43) or as the second term of the iḍāfa (26:192, 36:5). The maṣdar then stands for its object: Zamakhsharī 26:192 "التنزيل: المنزل"; al-Ālūsī 36:5 "المصدر بمعنى المفعول".';
